{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-3781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-3781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2{x}^{3}-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-3781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78dbd5e2a5fc03c6a49f5b6a945968accd3ed19fd2f255cd3ff5ae43841a69c6"} {"question" "q-3781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-3781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\infty "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-3781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-115528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-115528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-115528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78dbd5e2a5fc03c6a49f5b6a945968accd3ed19fd2f255cd3ff5ae43841a69c6"} {"question" "q-115528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-115528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\infty "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-115528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)}{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[ln\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)]}^{′}}{{[ln\\,(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x})}{(-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78dbd5e2a5fc03c6a49f5b6a945968accd3ed19fd2f255cd3ff5ae43841a69c6"} {"question" "q-336802" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336802" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{′}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{′}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78dbd5e2a5fc03c6a49f5b6a945968accd3ed19fd2f255cd3ff5ae43841a69c6"} {"question" "q-336903" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336903" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5){5}^{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5){e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78dbd5e2a5fc03c6a49f5b6a945968accd3ed19fd2f255cd3ff5ae43841a69c6"} {"question" "q-336927" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"} {"question" "q-336927" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\ln(5)}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052886/state.json" "file_sha256" "1fa699cc6162ec0c88ab74784141ef0f7560356fdf9ee5831a4ac30420dc076b"}] "usage" {"q-336927" [] "q-336903" []} "basis" 1770 "retractions" [[17592186259772 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{'}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{'}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186259775 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186259775 :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x]}^{'}}{[{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}5)}{2\\operatorname{ln}5\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}5)x}{{x}^{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}5} & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{5}^{x}-1]}^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}5){5}^{x}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}5){e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186259774 17592186259777] "immutable_fields" [[17592186259773 :answer-field/correct] [17592186259773 :answer-field/id] [17592186259773 :an